  "note": "Anti para / heliborne defence of a vulnerable point (GSP 1947 Inf Div in Battle s28 pp 127-133). The En may land to seize dominating ground, crossings or defiles, isolate a strong point, attack a formation HQ, block the entry of reserves, capture airfields or delay a withdrawal (para 2 p 127). The Comd finds the probable landing sites and likely objectives from the map and the ground (para 6 p 130): with the terrain the fit reads the likely landing areas (LA) round the VP (open ground a helicopter can land on, level, clear over a landing patch, nearest first; PlanDoctrine antiHb, from IpbDoctrine's LZ figures), else three are assumed 2 km out (E). Each LA under an OP (para 5 a pp 128-129), denied by obstacles (poles, stakes, mines, para 11 a p 131) and with a road block on the way to the VP (para 11 b p 131; para 13 a (5) p 132); a local defence force at the VP covering the landing areas and the objective (para 9 pp 130-131: a Coy, E); an immediate action group, mobile, to engage the En while landing (para 13 a p 132: a Pl, E) and a deliberate action group, a combined arms mobile force with armour, to destroy the En (para 10 p 131, para 13 b pp 132-133: a Coy group with tanks, E). At Div level reliance is on mobile reserves, the paucity of troops not permitting all landing areas to be held (para 4 p 128). Click the VP to guard.",
